# 71-luminos-uvm-gate.rules
# [CHANGE: claude-code | 2026-08-29] BUG-147 — close the boot-time world-open window on
# /dev/nvidia-uvm{,-tools}.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
# -----------------------
# nvidia-modprobe creates the two UVM nodes with a hardcoded 0666 root:root, and it is run
# from /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-nvidia.rules the moment the card binds — measured at t=6.87 s
# on this machine. luminos-uvm-gate.service did not run until t=9.09 s. That is a 2.2 second
# window at every single boot in which both nodes were readable and writable by everyone,
# i.e. DECISION 25 was not in force. Measured from the boot journal, 2026-08-29.
#
# WHY A UDEV RULE, WHEN THE GATE SCRIPT SAYS UDEV "CANNOT WORK HERE"
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# That comment is correct but narrower than it reads, and it cost a real amount of time.
# It is true of a KERNEL=="nvidia-uvm" match: nvidia-modprobe creates these nodes with
# mknod(2), which emits NO uevent, so udev never learns the node appeared and a rule keyed on
# the node itself can never fire. That is why 70-luminos-dgpu-access.rules has never once
# fired for anything.
#
# This rule is keyed on something else entirely: the PCI DEVICE add/bind event, which is a
# real uevent and is exactly what 60-nvidia.rules itself matches on. We are not waiting to be
# told the node appeared — we are riding the same event that CAUSES it to appear.
#
# ORDERING, WHICH IS THE WHOLE FIX — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
# --------------------------------------------------------
# udev parses rule files in lexical filename order and accumulates RUN+= commands in the
# order they were added, executing them after all rules for that event are parsed. 71 sorts
# after 60, so nvidia-modprobe runs first and this runs second. Verified 2026-08-29 by
# deleting both nodes and triggering an add: our RUN observed them as [root:root 666],
# i.e. nvidia-modprobe had already created them by the time we ran. Had we run first we
# would have seen them ABSENT — and that would have been a disaster, see below.
#
# RESIDUAL WINDOW, MEASURED OVER 3 RUNS
# -------------------------------------
#   /dev/nvidia-uvm         9-10 ms   (was 2.2 s)
#   /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools     18 ms   (was 2.2 s)
# Roughly a 120-240x reduction. It is NOT zero and this file must not claim that it is.
#
# WHY NOT CLOSE IT COMPLETELY BY PRE-CREATING THE NODES FIRST
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# Because that breaks the GPU outright. Look at 60-nvidia.rules: the guard TEST!="/dev/nvidia-uvm"
# covers BOTH of its RUN+= lines, not just the UVM one. So if /dev/nvidia-uvm already exists
# when that rule is evaluated, nvidia-modprobe is not run AT ALL — and /dev/nvidia0 and
# /dev/nvidiactl, which it also creates, are never created either.
# MEASURED 2026-08-29: with the UVM nodes present and the main nodes deleted, an add trigger
# brought back NEITHER, and nvidia-smi failed with "couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA
# driver". A rule numbered BELOW 60 that pre-creates these nodes would do exactly this at
# every boot. Do not write one. See luminos-uvm-gate.sh for the matching guard.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO luminos-uvm-gate.service
# ----------------------------------------
# The service is now a BACKSTOP, not the primary mechanism. This rule is the primary one.
# The service still runs at boot and catches the case where this rule did not fire at all
# (file removed, udev not reloaded, module loaded by some other path later).
#
# KILL SWITCH: sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/71-luminos-uvm-gate.rules && sudo udevadm control --reload
# The service will then go back to repairing the nodes at ~9 s, with the old 2.2 s window.

ACTION=="add|bind", ATTR{vendor}=="0x10de", ATTR{class}=="0x03[0-9]*", \
    DRIVER=="nvidia", \
    RUN+="/usr/local/bin/luminos-uvm-gate --udev"
